<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Codecov: Install and connect" id="Codecov-Installation">

    <primary-label ref="codecov"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>License: 10-day free trial, then <ui-path>Help | Register</ui-path></p>
        <p>Connect: <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Codecov</ui-path></p>
        <p>Requires: a Git repository that is activated on Codecov, and a Codecov API token</p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Install the Codecov plugin, start the trial or activate a license, and connect the project to Codecov with an API token.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Install the plugin, activate it and connect the project to Codecov.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Install the Codecov plugin in JetBrains IDEs, start the 10-day trial and connect your project to Codecov with an API token from your Codecov account.</web-summary>

    <p>
        The Codecov plugin reads the coverage that your CI uploads to Codecov.
        Install it, activate a license or the trial, and connect the project once.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Requirements" id="codecov-requirements">
        <list>
            <li>The current version works only with JetBrains IDEs 2026.3, from build 263.3889 (the 2026.3 EAP) on.
                It does not install on earlier or later major versions.
                For JetBrains IDEs 2026.2, the Marketplace provides Codecov 2026.2.5.</li>
            <li>The project is a Git repository with a remote on GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket.</li>
            <li>The repository is activated on Codecov, and your CI uploads coverage reports to it.
                To set this up, see <a href="https://docs.codecov.com/docs/quick-start">Codecov quick start</a>.</li>
            <li>A Codecov API token for an account that can access the repository.</li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Install the Codecov plugin" id="codecov_install">
        <procedure title="Install from JetBrains Marketplace" id="codecov-installation-procedure">
            <step>
                Open <ui-path>Settings | Plugins</ui-path> (<shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+S</shortcut>, macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ,</shortcut>).
            </step>
            <step>
                On the <control>Marketplace</control> tab, search for <code>Codecov</code>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>Install</control> and restart the IDE if prompted.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            You can also install the plugin from its <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/23390-codecov">JetBrains Marketplace page</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Start the trial or activate a license" id="codecov-license">
        <p>
            Codecov is a paid plugin with a 10-day free trial.
            Start the trial or activate a license in <ui-path>Help | Register</ui-path>.
            In free IDEs, such as Android Studio, use <ui-path>Help | Register Plugins</ui-path>.
            Buy or manage licenses on the <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/23390-codecov/pricing">Codecov plugin pricing page</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Connect the project to Codecov" id="codecov-sign-in">
        <p>
            The plugin authenticates with a Codecov API token.
            The settings page shows the hint "Generate a token at codecov.io/api/" under the token field.
        </p>

        <procedure title="Connect with an API token" id="sign-in-procedure">
            <step>
                In Codecov, create an API token on your personal account, not on an organization account:
                <list type="decimal">
                    <li>Click your avatar in the top right corner and select <control>Settings</control>.</li>
                    <li>In the left menu, select <control>Access</control>.</li>
                    <li>Click <control>Generate Token</control>, enter a name for the token and click <control>Generate</control>.</li>
                    <li>Copy the token.</li>
                </list>
                For details, see <a href="https://docs.codecov.com/reference/overview">Codecov API reference: getting started</a>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Open the project in the IDE.
            </step>
            <step>
                Open <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Codecov</ui-path> (<shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+S</shortcut>, macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ,</shortcut>).
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>Codecov Connection</control> group, keep <control>Server URL</control> at <code>https://api.codecov.io</code>.
                For a self-hosted Codecov server, enter the base URL of that server instead.
            </step>
            <step>
                In <control>Service Provider</control>, select where the repository is hosted: <control>Github</control>, <control>Gitlab</control> or <control>Bitbucket</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                If you changed <control>Server URL</control> or <control>Service Provider</control>, click <control>OK</control>, reopen the project and open <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Codecov</ui-path> again.
                Repository detection uses the new values only after you reopen the project.
            </step>
            <step>
                Paste the token into <control>API Token</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>Test Connection</control>.
                The plugin checks the token, finds the Codecov repository that matches the project's Git remote, and saves the token.
                <!-- planned-image src="codecov_connection_test_success.png" alt="Connection Test Successful dialog showing the detected repository" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                Close the result dialog and click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>

        <p>
            When the connection works, the settings page shows "Connected to Github: demo-org/demo-project" and "Detected Repository: demo-org/demo-project".
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="codecov_settings_connection.png" alt="Codecov settings page connected to a GitHub repository" -->

        <p>
            The next time you open the project, the plugin detects the repository again from the Git remotes.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Results of Test Connection" id="codecov-test-connection-results">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Connection Test Successful" id="test-result-success">
                <p>The dialog reads "Successfully connected to Codecov and detected repository: demo-org/demo-project".
                    Coverage, the Codecov Metrics dashboard and folder coverage are available.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Connection Test Successful, without a repository" id="test-result-no-repository">
                <p>A warning reads "Connected to Codecov but could not detect repository. Please ensure your repository is activated on Codecov."
                    The token works, but no Codecov repository matches the Git remote.
                    To fix it, see <a href="Codecov-FAQ.topic" anchor="no-repository-detected"/>.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Connection Test Failed" id="test-result-failed">
                <p>The dialog reads "Connection failed. Please check your token.", or shows the error returned by the server.
                    To fix it, see <a href="Codecov-FAQ.topic" anchor="auth-errors"/>.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Connect other projects" id="codecov-connect-other-projects">
        <p>
            Each project needs its own connection.
            A project that is not connected shows no coverage, even though the API token is saved for all projects.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Connect another project" id="codecov-connect-another-project">
            <step>
                Open the project and go to <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Codecov</ui-path>.
                <control>API Token</control> is already filled in.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>Test Connection</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Close the result dialog and click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            If you click <control>OK</control> without <control>Test Connection</control>, the project stays disconnected.
        </p>
        <p>
            <control>Service Provider</control> applies to all projects, so the plugin works with one provider at a time.
            To connect a project hosted on another provider, for example GitLab instead of GitHub, select that provider in <control>Service Provider</control>, change the API token if needed, and click <control>OK</control>.
            Then reopen the project and click <control>Test Connection</control> in its Codecov settings.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Disconnect or change the token" id="codecov-disconnect">
        <p>
            <control>Server URL</control>, <control>Service Provider</control> and the API token apply to all projects.
            The token is stored in the IDE password safe.
            The connection to a repository is stored per project.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Disconnect the project" id="codecov-disconnect-procedure">
            <step>
                Go to <ui-path>Settings | Tools | Codecov</ui-path>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>Disconnect</control>.
                The button is shown only while the project is connected.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            <control>Disconnect</control> removes the connection for the current project only and keeps the stored API token for other projects.
            The <control>API Token</control> field is then empty, but the plugin has no option to delete the stored token.
            To invalidate the token, revoke it in the <control>Access</control> settings of your Codecov account.
            To use a different token, paste it into <control>API Token</control> and click <control>Test Connection</control>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

